WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.593
In Lecture eighteen

2
00:00:04.677 --> 00:00:07.470
eigenvalues were the roots of the

3
00:00:07.555 --> 00:00:10.263
determinant of A minus lambda I.

4
00:00:10.263 --> 00:00:12.802
Here is a symmetric matrix S

5
00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:14.918
and the ellipse it makes

6
00:00:15.003 --> 00:00:16.780
from the unit circle.

7
00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.252
Its eigenvalues are the half axes

8
00:00:20.252 --> 00:00:22.731
about three point six two

9
00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:25.210
and one point three eight.

10
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.630
For a matrix with thousands of rows

11
00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.870
does a library solve that polynomial?

12
00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.320
Pause and guess.

13
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.042
It does not.

14
00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:41.469
Take a symmetric sixteen by sixteen

15
00:00:41.564 --> 00:00:44.991
matrix with eigenvalues ten, eleven

16
00:00:44.991 --> 00:00:49.180
up to twenty five, evenly spaced on a line.

17
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.330
Form its characteristic polynomial

18
00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.170
and ask for the roots.

19
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.650
Eight of the sixteen leave the

20
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.810
line and come out complex

21
00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.210
and the real parts are off by

22
00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.130
up to zero point seven.

23
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.593
Polynomial roots are extremely

24
00:01:05.664 --> 00:01:07.214
sensitive to rounding.

25
00:01:07.214 --> 00:01:10.032
The library never forms the polynomial

26
00:01:10.032 --> 00:01:12.920
and gets all sixteen to fourteen digits.

27
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.100
It uses the QR algorithm.

28
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.700
Factor A into Q times R

29
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.276
then multiply them back in the other order

30
00:01:23.276 --> 00:01:24.420
R times Q.

31
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.744
R Q equals Q transpose A Q: the same map

32
00:01:28.744 --> 00:01:31.071
seen in a turned frame.

33
00:01:31.071 --> 00:01:34.463
On the picture, the ellipse turns

34
00:01:34.463 --> 00:01:38.147
but its half axes keep their lengths

35
00:01:38.147 --> 00:01:40.570
so the eigenvalues stay.

36
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.437
What changes is the off diagonal entry

37
00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:46.963
from one to minus one half.

38
00:01:46.963 --> 00:01:50.480
It measures how tilted the ellipse is.

39
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.112
Now repeat.

40
00:01:52.208 --> 00:01:54.982
The tilt goes from thirty two

41
00:01:55.077 --> 00:01:58.617
degrees to minus thirteen, then five

42
00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:02.060
then minus two, then less than one.

43
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.536
Each step, the off diagonal entry shrinks by

44
00:02:05.607 --> 00:02:07.668
the ratio of the eigenvalues

45
00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:09.586
one point three eight over

46
00:02:09.657 --> 00:02:11.078
three point six two

47
00:02:11.078 --> 00:02:13.210
about zero point three eight.

48
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.105
On the three by three matrix in the notes

49
00:02:17.105 --> 00:02:19.974
the ratio is zero point four two

50
00:02:19.974 --> 00:02:23.350
and the corner needs thirty four steps.

51
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.207
Like power iteration

52
00:02:25.207 --> 00:02:28.920
it is only as fast as its eigenvalue ratio.

53
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.300
The cure is a shift.

54
00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.180
Subtract the corner entry times

55
00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.980
I before factoring

56
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.500
and add it back afterwards.

57
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.830
The result is still Q transpose A Q

58
00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.620
so the ellipse keeps its axes.

59
00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.727
Start again from S.

60
00:02:46.727 --> 00:02:50.057
One shifted step: thirteen degrees

61
00:02:50.057 --> 00:02:52.184
no better than before.

62
00:02:52.184 --> 00:02:54.959
The second: under one degree

63
00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:57.641
as good as four plain steps.

64
00:02:57.733 --> 00:03:00.323
The third leaves a corner of

65
00:03:00.415 --> 00:03:02.450
about five millionths.

66
00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.187
Back to the three by three matrix.

67
00:03:06.187 --> 00:03:07.765
Without a shift

68
00:03:07.765 --> 00:03:11.201
the corner needed thirty four steps.

69
00:03:11.201 --> 00:03:14.172
How many with the corner shift?

70
00:03:14.172 --> 00:03:15.750
Pause and guess.

71
00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:19.790
Five.

72
00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.381
The corner goes zero point seven five

73
00:03:23.381 --> 00:03:27.155
zero point two seven, seven thousandths

74
00:03:27.155 --> 00:03:28.720
a ten millionth

75
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.850
then ten to the minus twenty one.

76
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.104
Each bar counts the zeros

77
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.007
after the decimal point.

78
00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:37.987
Once the corner is small

79
00:03:37.987 --> 00:03:40.880
each step roughly triples that count.

80
00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.867
The corner entry is now an eigenvalue

81
00:03:43.867 --> 00:03:45.463
three plus root three.

82
00:03:45.463 --> 00:03:48.308
The algorithm drops that row and column

83
00:03:48.308 --> 00:03:50.390
and carries on with the rest.

84
00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.365
One more idea makes each step cheap.

85
00:03:54.365 --> 00:03:57.289
On a full five by five matrix

86
00:03:57.289 --> 00:04:01.250
a QR step costs about n cubed operations.

87
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.304
So first, just once

88
00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.969
Householder reflections on both sides zero

89
00:04:07.054 --> 00:04:10.377
everything below the first subdiagonal

90
00:04:10.377 --> 00:04:11.911
six entries here.

91
00:04:11.911 --> 00:04:15.320
This Hessenberg form H is similar to A.

92
00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:17.739
QR steps keep this shape

93
00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:21.463
and on it a step costs only about n squared.

94
00:04:21.546 --> 00:04:23.698
For a symmetric matrix the

95
00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.518
shape is tridiagonal

96
00:04:25.518 --> 00:04:27.670
and a step costs about n.

97
00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:30.729
But H is not triangular.

98
00:04:30.729 --> 00:04:35.377
Its diagonal starts minus four, almost zero

99
00:04:35.377 --> 00:04:37.650
one point eight four.

100
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.857
The eigenvalues are somewhere else

101
00:04:40.857 --> 00:04:42.408
two complex pairs

102
00:04:42.408 --> 00:04:44.612
and zero point nine three.

103
00:04:44.693 --> 00:04:47.142
Only the shifted QR steps that

104
00:04:47.224 --> 00:04:49.591
follow clear the subdiagonal

105
00:04:49.672 --> 00:04:52.039
and each complex pair settles

106
00:04:52.121 --> 00:04:54.080
into a two by two block.

107
00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.797
This is what NumPy does, through LAPACK

108
00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:59.734
Hessenberg form first

109
00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:01.670
then shifted QR steps.

110
00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.418
Now the whole course on one page.

111
00:05:05.418 --> 00:05:08.970
Each question has its factorization.

112
00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.690
To solve A x equals b: L U

113
00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:14.149
or for huge sparse systems

114
00:05:14.149 --> 00:05:15.992
conjugate gradients.

115
00:05:15.992 --> 00:05:18.889
When there is no exact solution

116
00:05:18.889 --> 00:05:22.840
Q R and the pseudoinverse give the best one.

117
00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.869
What a matrix does to directions

118
00:05:25.869 --> 00:05:27.330
its eigenvectors.

119
00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.359
What it does to lengths

120
00:05:29.359 --> 00:05:32.119
the singular value decomposition.

121
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.797
And the four subspaces say which

122
00:05:34.878 --> 00:05:37.070
right sides can be reached.

123
00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:38.982
Working with AI.

124
00:05:39.065 --> 00:05:42.977
An assistant claims that two conjugate gradient

125
00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.890
steps solve any two by two system.

126
00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:48.055
Test it on this matrix N

127
00:05:48.055 --> 00:05:50.052
which is not symmetric.

128
00:05:50.052 --> 00:05:52.050
The answer is one, one.

129
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.500
Two steps give about two point eight

130
00:05:55.586 --> 00:05:57.997
five and one point four six.

131
00:05:57.997 --> 00:05:58.599
Wrong.

132
00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:02.043
Conjugate gradients rely on a symmetric

133
00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.282
positive definite matrix

134
00:06:04.282 --> 00:06:07.640
check that before you trust the claim.

135
00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.040
To sum up.

136
00:06:09.122 --> 00:06:11.499
Every QR step turns the frame

137
00:06:11.581 --> 00:06:13.713
and keeps the eigenvalues.

138
00:06:13.795 --> 00:06:16.254
Shifting by the corner removes

139
00:06:16.336 --> 00:06:18.303
the tilt in a few steps

140
00:06:18.385 --> 00:06:21.255
and a one time Hessenberg reduction

141
00:06:21.337 --> 00:06:23.140
makes each step cheap.

142
00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:24.708
Now you try

143
00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:28.972
do one QR step by hand on the matrix two

144
00:06:28.972 --> 00:06:30.494
one, one, two

145
00:06:30.494 --> 00:06:34.250
and check that the trace stays four.

146
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.859
That completes the core course.

147
00:06:36.859 --> 00:06:38.389
Next, the electives

148
00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:40.720
starting with graph Laplacians.
